The system calls and library functions provided by Linux follow
very closely the IEEE POSIX.1 standard, therefore this is an
important reference for system and application Linux hackers.

If you want to buy a copy of the POSIX spec, you might be interested
in the following:

IEEE and ISO have recently published the 1996 revision of
POSIX.1. This new edition now incorporates the extensions for
real-time support (POSIX.1b, POSIX.1i) and threads (POSIX.1c):

  ISO/IEC 9945-1:1996 (ANSI/IEEE Std 1003.1, 1996 Edition),
  Information technology--Portable Operating System Interface (POSIX)
  Part 1: System Application Program Interface (API) [C Language],
  896 pages, ISBN 1-55937-573-6, IEEE order number SH94352-NYF.
  Price: 120 USD (first copy for IEEE members: 84 USD).

You can order this standard as well as all other IEEE standards
directly from IEEE:

  phone:  +1 908 981 1393 (TZ: eastern standard time)
           1 800 678 4333 (toll free from US+Canada only)
  fax:    +1 908 981 9667
  e-mail: stds.info@ieee.org

Information about POSIX and other IEEE standards is available on
<URL:http://stdsbbs.ieee.org/> and some information about POSIX on
<URL:http://www.knosof.co.uk/posix.html>. Unfortunately the full
standard documents are only available as books or on CD-ROM, not on
the Internet. Having access to the POSIX specs is certainly a good
idea for any Linux kernel hacker.

More information about the POSIX.1b real-time extensions and their
current implementation status under Linux is available on 

  ftp.informatik.uni-erlangen.de/local/cip/mskuhn/misc/linux-posix.1b

