Linux Newsletter:              Issue #2                Date: 01/01/97

In this issue:

- - Linux show on PCTV 
- - Linux presentation to Trade Press and SoftBank Comdex
- - USELINUX Show, Anaheim,  Jan 6-10, 1997 
- - Uniforum Show, San Francisco, March 10-14, 1997, need Vendors & Volunteers
- - SoftBank Comdex participation Spring and Fall, Linux Vendors needed.
- - Beowulf Project Link
- - Inside SCO UNIX & Jim Mohr: retraction

===

New Year's greetings!  As you can see by the date, I never stop
working for Linux.  I sincerely hope that all of you had a very happy 
Holiday, and that this coming year turns into the best year you 
ever had!

===

- - Linux show on PCTV 

As some of you already know, PCTV just did a series on UNIX, and also
a show specifically on Linux.   This show was part of their popular
"Users Group" series shown throughout the world with 40 million
potential viewers.  You can find more information about PCTV at
http://www.pctv.com, and the site is under construction... aren't
they all?

Jon "Maddog" Hall arranged with PCTV for the Linux segment to be taped 
on Monday Dec 9th.  I drove to New Hampshire where the studio is 
(arriving in what they said was the worst snowstorm in years), and there 
met Maddog (Executive Director of Linux International), Ransom Love  
(Caldera), Bob Young (Red Hat Software), Brett Halle (Apple [MkLinux]) 
and also Jim Paradis & Jay Estabrook (Digital).

We all had a rollicking and very enjoyable dinner with Giles Bateman 
(Producer & Host), and Tom Schauer (Production Associate) of PCTV
educating them and each other about Linux.  This was held at the Eagle
Inn at Coit Mountain in Newport NH.  It is a beautiful facility and
I'd highly recommend staying there if you are in the area.

Then we all proceeded to put together a Linux Show on the following day.  
It was a lot of fun.  I never realized before that it takes an entire day 
to put together a half hour TV show.

Oh, and about that snowstorm.... I thought the worst part was having to
get out of the car and wipe off the road signs to see where I was... but
then I grew up in Alaska, and I have an All-Wheel drive van that I just
love.  Besides, I have driven the Alaska highway about 25 times, and
this was a picnic by comparison...

=

Here is a note from Tom Schauer (Production Associate at PCTV)

>I just wanted to drop you a quick note of thanks for appearing on our
>Cable TV computer show "User Group".  I know our viewers will find the 
>show both enjoyable and very informative! Thank you for doing such 
>outstanding work.
>
>I also wanted to let you know that the "UNIX III - Linux" show will air 
>on the Jones Computer Network (JCN) and the Mind Extension University 
>Channel (MEU) the week of January 20, 1997.
>
>The scheduled times are:
>Mon. 11:30 PM - 12:00 AM  Wed.  9:30 PM - 10:00 PM  Thu. 11:30 PM - 12:00 AM
>Fri.  9:30 PM - 10:00 PM  Sun.  9:30 PM - 10:00 PM
>This show will also air on the NBC Superchannel (CNBC) January 25, 1997.
>It is best to call your local cable operator to find the appropriate channel. 

I included the above because the show schedule, and links to the Linux
Vendor's sites do not yet appear to be on the PCTV web site, and I thought
that you would want to know when you could see the show.  Thank you Tom!

I think that Giles & Tom did a great job on this Linux show, and I look
forward to hearing Feedback about it.
 
===

- - Linux presentation to Trade Press and SoftBank Comdex

People look at me strangely and say "You live in Colorado, you are crazy
to drive to the east coast".  While that may be true.... I find it much
more convenient with the frequent schedule changes, the amount of equipment
and product I carry, among other reasons to drive than to fly.  Besides
it give me uninterrupted thinking time that is mine and mine alone.

Actually I find there is another benefit in driving.  After I am finished 
with the reason I went to a particular area, such as a Trade Show. I always 
find it useful to be able to schedule appearances for User Groups on the 
way out or back, and also to stop and visit with members of the trade press,
and demonstrate Linux for them.  This has resulted in a lot of stories
being written already that would not have been otherwise.

On this particular trip I went to Boston and visited with Christine Burns 
(Senior Writer at Network World), and Susan Mael (New Products Editor 
for Software Magazine).  Both were impressed with Linux and said 
they would find ways to mention it favorably in future articles.

I also stopped in at SoftBank Comdex and with the help of Ted Prindle 
(Show Director Uniforum), presented a demonstration of Linux 
to Bill Sell (Show Director Comdex), and Bill Mahan (Senior
Director GSA/Conference Sales) who were very impressed.  They both 
see Linux taking an increasingly larger role in future SoftBank Comdex 
events and wish to help in any way that they can.  You can see 
http://www.comdex.com for more information.  They were very hospitable 
and helpful. Both had taken to heart the comment I made in the last 
issue of this newsletter about the signage & promotion not getting 
done for the Linux Pavilion at Comdex Fall/96.  Already SoftBank has 
clicked into planning mode for Linux at Uniforum'97, Comdex Spring/97, 
and Comdex Fall/97 and we the Linux community need to support SoftBank's
efforts in every way that we can.  Maddog (as he prefers to be called)
and I are working closely with Softbank on planning.  

If you are a Linux Vendor, please contact Maddog (maddog@zk3.dec.com) 
to join as a sponsor or a member of Linux International, and let us 
(Maddog or I) know which shows you want to attend (SoftBank or otherwise).

===

- - USELINUX Show, Anaheim,  Jan 6-10, 1997 

As long as I am on the Topic of Shows....

In case you are unaware of it, USELINUX, the  Linux Applications 
Development and Deployment Conference will be taking place in 
Anaheim January 6-10, along with the USENIX Technical Conference.  
USELINUX features developers and business tracks, tutorials, and 
invited talks.  There will be presentations and case studies where 
you will find out how things really work from speakers with real-life 
experience.  There is also a "Vendor Booth" trade show.  USELINUX is 
sponsored by Linux International and the USENIX Association.  For 
more information, visit the USENIX Web site:  
http://www.usenix.org, or contact the conference office 
at 714.588.8649  .... submitted by Zanna Knight

I'll see you there!

===

