===

- - SoftBank Comdex participation Spring and Fall, Linux Vendors needed.

More will be said about this as soon as we have the details organized,
but it is not too early for Linux Vendors to call Softbank and specify
that you wish to reserve a booth in the Linux Pavilion.  One of the 
things I asked SoftBank show management was whether they would be 
interested in asking Linus Torvalds to be a keynoter.  They said yes,
IF the Linux community can meet the criteria that they set for other
people that they ask to be keynoters. 

1) The person must be a well known industry figure, which Linus is,
   although not yet to the mainstream, and
2) The person should represent an organization that will draw in 
   a substantial amount of Vendors and Attendees, as well as have
   the press be interested in coming to hear him speak.  We in the 
   Linux community have not yet proved that this is the case.  More 
   vendors and more attendees as a result of our activities at future 
   shows are what will bring this recognition about.

SoftBank did however mention that they would be interested in putting
on a Comdex Special Linux Program of presentations if we would like.
These would get promoted by SoftBank as official functions.  I was
also told that Comdex Fall is indeed the largest computer show in the
world in terms of the number of people that come.  CeBit counts each
person multipled by the number of days they stay.  If Comdex did this
the numbers would be much higher than CeBit.

Even without Linus as a Keynoter (for now, unless we instantly get
huge numbers of people & companies signing up), between information 
about Linux in an attendee mailer, the Linux Pavilion and it's promotion, 
the Conference presentations and it's promotion, and other benefits 
that they are willing to provide, Linux can grow significantly, as can 
Comdex as a result of the LI/SoftBank relationship.

Why am I lobbying so hard for Linux at Comdex/Uniforum?  They are 
Mainstream computer shows, and if the press cannot avoid seeing Linux,
it will result in mainstream recognition for Linux, which can only 
benefit us all.

===

- - Beowulf Project Link

For those of you that have not heard of the Beowulf Linux project, you
should check out the following URL submitted by Philip W. Dabney of the
NASA/Goddard Space Flight Center (Thanks Phil, and look out Cray!)
http://cesdis.gsfc.nasa.gov/linux-web/beowulf/beowulf.html

===
- - Inside SCO Unix & Jim Mohr: retraction

Retraction:
In Issue 1 of this newsletter, we reported that Jim Mohr is the author
of "Inside SCO Unix".  That proves to be incorrect.  Jim wrote articles
for "Inside SCO Unix Systems Magazine", and the book he wrote is the
"SCO Companion: The Essential Guide for Users and Systems Administrators"
aka "The SCO Companion".  

As long as we are on this topic, Jim got lots of valuable input, but there 
is a glaring lack of "reference sites", namely large deployments of Linux 
to solve a "Business" problem or "Strategic" problems within a company that 
are referenceable by other companies wishing to do similar deployments.  
There were very few "vertical market" applications, where extensive customer 
deployments of strategic systems have been done.  We know such sites exist, 
but most refuse to provide references on the grounds of "strategic advantage" 
or on the grounds that their projects have not yet been running long enough
that their attorneys feel comfortable using it as PR.  If anyone knows of
such sites who'll talk (especially management types), please step forward. 

One thing we did learn, is that the world abounds with "Linux as a server",
or as a programming station.

