After an update of the entire contents of the Linux Projects Map (LPM) in
mid-December I decided to introduce a new and better format to reflect the
changed requirements of today. The LPM is now entirely produced with the
help of the sgml-tools package (former linuxdoc-sgml). For submitters, this
has the advantage of a flexible format without the restirctions of the old
static format. A sample entry to be submitted might look as follows: 

BEGIN LPM ENTRY

<tag/Title:/Your Project Title here

<tag/Description:/A short description of the
project, what it is, what it's good for etc. The less
you write the more probably it is it get's read.

<tag/Author:/Your name(s)

<tag/Author's E-Mail:/Your e-mail address(es) in
URL format, e.g. <htmlurl url="mailto:hm@seneca.muc.de"
name="hm@seneca.muc.de">

<tag/Information Sources:/Any sources of
information you can think of - FTP and WWW servers
etc. - in URL format if possible

<tag/Assistance required:/whether or not you
require assistance, special skills etc.

<tag/Current Status:/Alpha, Beta, whatever

<tag/ETA:/ Your estimated release date

<tag/Remarks:/Anything else that might be useful
to know, e.g. specific packages that are needed to
compile/run your software.

END LPM ENTRY

That means although you have the flexibility to write virtually what you
want you don't necessarily need to be a sgml-tools guru. If you don't use
URLs you don't need to know anything about the Linuxdoc DTD at all. 

The new LPM can be seen at http://www.muc.de/~hm/linux/Projects-Map.html.
The copy on sunsite is not yet in place due to problems with ncftpd... But
it will be there in http://sunsite.unc.edu/pub/Linux/docs/Projects-Map/
hopefully soon. 

