KeyKit is a system for MIDI experimentation, both algorithmic and realtime.
It includes a multi-tasking programming language designed specifically for
MIDI, and a multi-window graphical user interface with pop-up/pull-off
menus and a variety of graphical tools, including a complete multi-track
editor with a large number of editing operations and infinite undo.
The gui is completely implemented (even the multi-window manager and
pull-off buttons) with the KeyKit language in a user-defined library,
so you can add new tools, and add new editing operations to the multi-track
editor, even while the system is running.

AT&T is making KeyKit freely available (including complete source code)
for non-commercial use.  The download site is <http://www.nosuch.com/keykit>.
In addition to source code, screen dumps, and documentation, the site
contains executables for Linux and Win95.  Send email to tjt@nosuch.com
if you have any questions.  My home page is <http://www.nosuch.com>,
where you'll find a collection of MIDI files for music that I've composed
using KeyKit exclusively.  You'll also find a web-based musical toy
called Muse-O-Matic - you enter a word and get back algorithmic music
based on it, generated with KeyKit code.

     ...Tim Thompson...tjt@nosuch.com....

