PRESENTATION DESCRIPTION
========================
Kevin Ruland is a research engineer at Systems & Electronics Inc 
working with computer vision systems.  He received a Doctor of 
Science in Applied Mathematics from Washington University in 1995.  
His first exposure to Linux was kernel 0.99pl15 with the sole 
purpose of TeX-ing his dissertation.

Because Linux systems have a very favorable price/performance ratio, 
many engineers, scientists and mathematicians are using Linux as 
their primary research tool.  A great variety of powerful mathematical 
tools are available for the Linux platform.  Kevin will discuss the
commercial and freeware software for symbolic algebra, statistics,
numerical analysis, and simulation.

Some of the software Kevin will discuss is listed below:

  Numerical Packages:
    Matlab, MuPad, SciLab, ...

  Algebraic Packages:
    Mathematica, Maple V, PUNIMAX

  Statistical Analysis & Data Visualization:
    Partek, ...

