out.  Send reservations to aftyde@aol.com.   Please let us know in 
advance if you require a special menu.  

RSVP for DINNER, MEETING or BOTH to aftyde@aol.com

Topical Abstract (edited down for this announcement)

PRACTICAL PERFORMANCE PREDICTION

Dr. Neil Gunther
Computer Dynamics Consulting
Mountain View, California
<ngunther@ricochet.net>
<http://members.aol.com/CoDynamo/Home.htm>

"Performance prediction is especially important when procuring, or 
configuring computers.  Often, however, this part of  system design is 
skipped.  When it comes to computer performance, many operators fly blind 
(or at least in a fog).  Predicting computer performance requires 
knowledge of"

Dr. Gunther will address issues relative to the planning and performance of

   o Multiple processor (modeled as a MultiServer queue)
   o Dual computers (modeled as Parallel Queues)
   o Single CPU (modeled as a UniServer queue)

In single and multi-user environments- relative to differing load 
conditions.  

"You can find many books on this notoriously difficult subject but they 
are usually written BY mathematicians FOR mathematicians. What they never 
tell you is that it doesn't have to be that way. So, that's my task today."

Once again- if you have any role in planning for systems this is your 
chance to hear a world class expert speak clearly and comprehensibly on 
this esoteric subject.

Our Speaker

Dr. Neil J. Gunther was born in Melbourne, Australia. He holds 
undergraduate degrees in Chemistry and Physics, an M.Sc. in Applied 
Mathematics from La Trobe University, Australia, and a Ph.D. in 
Theoretical Physics from the University of Southampton, England. As an 
undergraduate, he became determined to ignore computers after dropping 
punch-cards containing his molecular orbital calculations for a PDP-8.

On arriving in the "silicon valley" in 1980, at the dawn of the PC 
revolution, he went from ignoring computers to building one. Nonetheless, 
in the ensuing sixteen years, his physical sciences background has 
provided valuable "ignorance" for understanding how computers really work.

Dr. Gunther spent eight years as a researcher in at the Xerox Palo Alto 
Research Center; his postdoctoral education in Computer Science. At Xerox 
PARC, he developed the "PARCbench" multiprocessor benchmark (an early 
SPEC benchmark contender), and he also developed the path integral 
method  (borrowed from quantum mechanics) for analyzing large transients 
in computer systems and networks. Other work at PARC included VLSI design 
and test for the Dragon multiprocessor workstation (now reincarnated as 
the SPARCcenter multiprocessor servers from Sun Microsystems).

In 1990 Dr. Gunther joined Pyramid Technology (now a subsidiary of 
Siemens Nixdorf). There, he held positions as Senior Scientist and 
Manager of the Performance Analysis Group. In that capacity he helped 
Pyramid attain industry high TP1 and TPC benchmarks on its Unix 
multiprocessor products. He also did performance simulation studies for 
the design of the RM1000 massively parallel database server.

In 1994 Dr. Gunther founded Computer Dynamics Consulting whose clients 
have included: Amdahl  Corporation, Tandem Computers, and the Transaction 
Processing Performance Council. Computer Dynamics also offers training 
courses and workshops in distributed systems performance management and 
capacity planning. Dr. Gunther lectures worldwide and has written over 50 
articles on a wide variety of topics in computer performance analysis.

Most recently he was awarded Best Technical Paper at CMG'96. Dr. Gunther 
is a member of the ACM and the IEEE.

