Linux version 2.1.14 running natively on the PowerPC platform is being
released in Beta form.

It is available via:

ftp://ftp.nmt.edu:/pub/people/cort/
http://www.cs.nmt.edu/~linuxppc/

The source, a filesytem tar file, bootimage, setup kernel and installation
instructions are all there.

Currently the system runs on the IBM 830 and Motorola PowerStack.  Other
architectures, including the PowerMac, are being worked on.

The system can boot from floppy, network and harddisk.  It is
self-compiling, self-hosting and can run the standard suite of Linux
programs (emacs, shell utilities, gcc, etc).  X clients work but the
server runs on only the IBM (built around the S3 chip) at the moment.
The system is still being written and is in development stages but is quite
stable.

The work builds upon that done by Gary Thomas and offers faster memory
management and interrupt handling.

For more information see http://www.cs.nmt.edu/~linuxppc/ or email
cort@cs.nmt.edu.

Thanks to IBM and Motorola for the donation of hardware.

