   CONTENTS
1. Short description of The Product
2. Installation
3. Usage
4. Bugs/features

1. SHORT DESCRIPTION
   wwwdrag v1.0 is blazingly fast Web search engine built around dog slow
   postgres95 database (where, where is Oracle for Linux ??).
   wwwdrag v.1.0 is scanning Web html documents for a given search_string
   and stores the findings in postgres95 database. Due to the limitations
   of postgres95, this release does not store the whole documents
   but only their urls and titles. Specifically, it stores the urls and
   titles of all documents visited plus it stores the count of how
   many times the search_string was found in the given document.
   The pre-built postgres95 database comes with The Product.
   The results of the search(es) are stored in the following tables:
   url			contains url_id, port, hostname, and full path
   url_title		contains url_id and title
   search_counts	contains url_id, string_id, count, etime
   			where count is the count of how many times
			search string with string_id was found in
			url with url_id, and etime is abstime in
			seconds when this url was visited last time
   search_strings	contains string_id and string to search for.
   Poking around database with psql monitor which comes with
   postgres can be an excellent way to start learning SQL.

[ Installation and usage removed by moderator.  --liw ]

4. BUGS OR RATHER FEATURES
   1) wwwdrag does not care about case/spaces/tabs/newlines in search strings:
      search strings
      "Linux" "linux" "L I N U X" are all the same for wwwdrag
   2) expect your hard drive to scream when you do a search,
      especially if you're directly connected to Internet.
      This is because postgres flushes buffers after every transaction,
      and the sucker does not cache the queries.
   3) wwwdrag will skip all documents with
     "jpg" "gif" "map" "jpeg" "gz" "tar" extensions.
   4) this release supports http protocol only. Urls with gopher://
      ftp://  mailto: or news: will be skipped.
   5) postgres95 has a limit of 8192 bytes for query buffer,
      hence it's not a good idea to store whole html documents in
      postgres tables.
   6) postgres95 does not support bind variables in a query;
      postgres95 does not support composite indices;
      postgres95 does not cache queries;

